  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure will support tranquil weather\nthrough Tuesday.\n\nA vertically stacked area of low pressure remains centered over\nsouthwestern Quebec. This ties to a occluded frontal system\nwhich extends along the New England coast into the eastern\nCarolinas. While characterized by 500-mb height departures on\nthe order of 2 to 2.5 standard deviations below climatology, any\ndirect impacts remain off to the north. For the local area,\ndaytime heating has fostered the development of a stratocumulus\ndeck. Although the GOES-19 visible channel does show some\nenhancement on satellite near I-95, there is quite a bit of dry\nair in the 700-500 mb layer which is limiting cloud top \nheights. The forecast will remain dry amidst this mix of clouds\nand sun. With enough boundary layer mixing, westerly winds \nshould top out in the 15 to 20 mph range this afternoon.\nForecast highs top out in the upper 70s to mid 80s, with mainly\n60s to mids 70s for mountain locations.\n\nGiven dew points are in the 50s to low 60s, this dry air mass \nwill support a decent cooling trend into the evening and \novernight period. Winds remain light overnight as an anticyclone\napproaches from the Ohio Valley. Coupled with mainly clear\nskies, ample radiational cooling effects are likely which will\nfavor 50s to low 60s.\n\nThe dome of high pressure remains in charge on Tuesday while the\nearlier front stays along the southeastern U.S. coast. This does\ncome with an uptick in temperatures as the upper low lifts\npoleward which yields a height/thickness rise. Northwesterly\ngradients persist which helps maintain the lower humidity air\nmass. High temperatures on Tuesday are expected to top out in\nthe low/mid 80s (upper 60s to 70s in the mountains). Tuesday\nnight's lows will rise a bit given some added cloud cover.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Turning more warm and humid through Thursday, \nwith increasing thunderstorm chances, before high pressure \nreturns for the weekend.\n\nThe early week surface anticyclone begins to depart off the\nAtlantic coast by Wednesday. Clockwise flow around this exiting\nhigh pressure will usher in a southerly wind through the day.\nSome afternoon gusts up to 20 mph are possible depending on the\ndegree of vertical mixing. High temperatures begin to approach\nthe 90 degree mark, which comes with a gradual uptick in dew\npoints/humidity levels. This is accompanied by the expected\nincrease in overnight warmth.\n\nAn upper trough currently crossing southern Saskatchewan is\nlikely to impact the local weather by Wednesday and Thursday.\nWhile much of Wednesday should be dry, some weak/modest\nperturbations in the flow ahead of the trough could bring some \nlate day showers. However, the more notable forcing from this\ntrough does not arrive until Thursday. A mixture of\nthermodynamic ingredients are in place for this event, but it is\ndifficult to say if these will align or not. While instability\nlevels increase with the uptick in low-level moisture, vertical\nshear generally averages around 20 to 25 knots. Some isolated\nsevere thunderstorms are possible, but the overall setup lends\nitself to a number of question marks. However, Thursday does\nlook like a typical late August day with a front encroaching on\nthe region. This would be characterized by numerous showers and\nsome thunderstorms, many of which will bring heavy downpours and\nfrequent lightning. Any thunderstorm threat may linger into the\nnight with the cold front being a late arriver.\n\nHeading into Friday and the weekend, an upper-level trough \nbegins to move directly over our forecast area. With highs \nremaining in the mid 70s to 80s, warm and humid conditions \nreturn to our region. This airmass brings with it chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms Friday afternoon and evening. \n\nOn Saturday and Sunday, the upper air wave pattern flattens into \nzonal flow. Depending on whether this synoptic pattern holds, \nSaturday and Sunday could see a few diurnally driven showers \nand thunderstorms.",
